import{_ as t,o,c as s,a3 as a}from"./chunks/framework.1Y430-yj.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"A Stable Overflow Router for Unstable Flow","description":"","frontmatter":{"lang":"en-US","translationSource":{"title":"针对不稳定流量的稳定溢流器","archivedAt":"2026-10-23T14:20:34.857Z","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=ifqEe9XTNdBTG1goxN6xkD","scope":"page"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"logistics/src/page-ifqEe9XTNdBTG1goxN6xkD.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-ifqEe9XTNdBTG1goxN6xkD.md","lastUpdated":null}'),l={name:"logistics/src/page-ifqEe9XTNdBTG1goxN6xkD.md"};function r(i,e,n,c,d,p){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="针对不稳定流量的稳定溢流器" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="a-stable-overflow-router-for-unstable-flow" tabindex="-1">A Stable Overflow Router for Unstable Flow <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-stable-overflow-router-for-unstable-flow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A Stable Overflow Router for Unstable Flow&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>ifqEe9XTNdBTG1goxN6xkD</code> ｜ <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=ifqEe9XTNdBTG1goxN6xkD" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>When stashes transfer materials wirelessly and depot stock is insufficient, Unloaders output fluctuating flow rather than full rate. Below, wireless transfer returns an average 2 belts (60/s) to the depot, with three Unloaders and conventional overflow routers attempting to recombine onto two belts. Protocol Stashes transfer every 10 seconds, here 10 items at once. These 10 items briefly make three Unloaders run full-rate (6 seconds, 9 items), so the router receives three items simultaneously and overflows, defeating the intention.</p><p><img src="/endfield-logistics-wiki/logistics/src/assets/images/img-66d2e406f5f70c3a.q90-d13d2172ea61595d.webp" alt="Stable Overflow Router for Unstable Flow"></p><p>Below is currently the most stable tested flow-stabilizing overflow router. Input remains 60/s through Protocol Stashes. Splitters prioritize belts from the upper two Unloaders, then “top up” those main belts from the lowest Unloader. Since the lower-right stash accepts at most 30/s, no overflow occurs while unblocked, achieving the original intention.</p><p>If input rises to 90/s, the unpowered stash accumulates inventory, eventually blocks, and overflows, diverting excess to other lines.</p><p><img src="/endfield-logistics-wiki/logistics/src/assets/images/img-6313ae04a51a65ae.q90-8d4e1b4eaa1ca261.webp" alt="Stable Overflow Router for Unstable Flow"></p><p>Extension 1: the unpowered stash buffers instantaneous input. Even average 60/s delivered as 120 items every 120 seconds can be buffered. Could belts replace it? The lowest Unloader outputs at most four items in one burst in the example; would a 4-tile belt work?</p><p>The tested device below uses only 3 tiles and runs stably online/offline. One problem:</p><p>After traveling from the Valley to Wuling, playing for a while, and returning, the lower-left stash gained inventory and strange overflow occurred. Perhaps state changes altered priority (the layout was already questionable).</p><p><img src="/endfield-logistics-wiki/logistics/src/assets/images/img-32002fa6865f8766.q90-1d7c7108a728b890.webp" alt="Stable Overflow Router for Unstable Flow"></p><p>Extension 2: a more compact layout, with the unpowered stash occupying the main belt as below, seems possible...? Actually it works only below 75/s throughput. With three full belts, the unpowered stash eventually accumulates inventory and blocks the upper two main routes. Total flow falls below three belts; full-load overflow underperforms expectations.</p><p><img src="/endfield-logistics-wiki/logistics/src/assets/images/img-4af45990bfddcb6c.q90-25e413706d8477ad.webp" alt="Stable Overflow Router for Unstable Flow"></p><p>Extension 3: can one main route and two overflow routes be made compact and stable?</p><blockquote><p>Translation note: The source repeatedly writes 60/s, 30/s, 90/s, and 75/s although its belt/batch examples appear to use per-minute rates. Units are preserved, not silently corrected. Image text is unchanged.</p></blockquote></div>',1)])])}const h=t(l,[["render",r]]);export{f as __pageData,h as default};
